So where does Growth sit in the organization? That's what we're going to focus on in this lesson. We're going to understand how while growth can be a distinct team, it's more about this disciplined approach, and it's important to ensure it expands across different departments. We're also going to understand the different ways to structure your growth team, covering three different approaches, and how you can make sure that they work best alongside other teams in a growth-centric approach. So let's dive in.

Growth comes from many different areas. It isn't one area. It comes from product, marketing, brand, data, company culture, strategy, operations—every single part of the organization is in some way responsible for growth. To have a growth approach means to have this very disciplined and ruthless focus on just a few areas as a cross-functional team, experimenting and learning about what does and doesn't work. That we have this flexibility and agility in our approach, switching quickly when things aren't working and testing quickly. And that with everything we do, we are focused on the end customer and the data that drives decisions.

For example, take an organization like this. This is based on a story that a fellow Growth lead told me about a company he was working for. He was working for an electric car lease brand, and they were struggling with the supply side. Here we can see where growth can come from operations because, due to the limited production of chips, they just didn't have enough cars to lease. As a result, the only way they could grow was to focus on the supply side and solve the problem there. For example, finding new supply partners and then building out a strong system with customers for marketing and products so that when supply is available, they can minimize the time between it arriving and going to the customer.

So even though they could focus on building an endless waitlist, there wasn't really a point. Growth was already strong because of the nature of the product. They were offering electric cars that were high quality for a good price. People were very positive about it. They had enough customers. Instead, they focused on additional upsells to get more out of existing customers. For example, offering higher mileage packages and breakdown insurance if they wanted to drive abroad, and other add-ons. This actually helped them grow fast by creating additional revenue drivers, allowing growth even though supply was limited. Here we see how important operations is for growth, and how we can grow through different revenue drivers versus just thinking in terms of marketing or product.

For our second example, again based on a conversation I've had and something I've seen play out, we see that growth can also come from better customer service. A vintage clothing store was struggling with high return rates and low conversion rates. As a result, they decided to add a chat function on their website to ask if people wanted additional support. They ensured that they always had someone online during key hours to be able to respond very quickly and have a real conversation rather than relying on back-and-forth emails. This allowed them to help customers quickly and also work out the key challenges they were facing.

It turned out that size and quality were the main issues. Customers weren’t sure what the sizes were because the store stocked many different brands that all used different sizing systems. They also weren’t sure about the quality—what did "good quality" really mean? Sometimes what they thought was good quality wasn’t what they received, and as a result, they were returning it. As part of the Growth team, these findings were discussed. In the short term, this helped reduce returns by giving customers valuable insights. But in the long term, this feedback fed into the product roadmap, highlighting which areas needed improvement. Again, we see that growth involves many different people and that it’s important to think about growth holistically, not just in terms of product or marketing.

There are a few different types of growth teams. Everyone always wants to know the best structure, but honestly, it depends on your organization. We won’t go into great depth on growth teams here, but I want you to see what growth-focused team structures can look like.

The first is the independent team. Here, we have a Head of Growth leading a team separate from other departments. This is purely an example; which departments and who is in the team will vary. The key thing is that it’s a multidisciplinary team reporting directly to the Head of Growth. This structure is great for speed and focus, but it can be difficult for an early-stage organization because there may not be full-time work for each team member, and the factors driving growth may change, requiring frequent team restructuring.

The second is a cross-functional model. Here, the team spans multiple departments. Team members report to their own department heads but collaborate as one team. This allows the organization to use its existing structure while still getting a diverse group of people involved. You also don’t need a separate lead for this model; instead, a Growth Product Manager or similar role can report directly into one of the departments.

The final is the mixed model, which is a combination of the two. Here, we have elements of the cross-functional model, where the team spans across different departments, but there is also a separate growth department. This doesn’t mean the entire growth team works in that department, but rather that some key members are housed within it while still collaborating across functions. Team members report to their department heads but work together as a cross-functional team.

So who is in the growth team, and who is working towards growth? Many different departments are involved. These are just a few examples to help you consider who might be part of your growth approach. You might have a separate growth department, particularly if you're using the mixed model structure. Marketing, product, operations, customer success, sales, business development, and brand often contribute valuable insights. Finance, legal, and compliance may also be involved.

A wide range of roles contribute to growth. Of course, job titles vary by organization, but here are some examples: Data analysts provide insights, customer care is key for gathering feedback, designers and developers help execute initiatives, and sales teams (in B2B organizations) play a major role. Copywriters, SEO specialists, legal experts, and brand managers are also important. Brand managers, in particular, should be involved—not to follow the growth process for their tasks but to support the brand side of the organization.

